---
.title = "Codegen",
.description = "Print an AST back to JavaScript or TypeScript source, with configurable formatting, type stripping, minification, and Source Map V3 output.",
.author = "Arshad Yaseen",
.date = @date("2026-06-23T00:00:00"),
.layout = "page.shtml",
---
The codegen prints an AST back to source in a single walk, so the output is always syntactically valid and faithful to the tree's structure. The JavaScript and Zig printers produce [byte-identical output](/testing/#codegen), source maps included.

## [JavaScript]($section.id('javascript'))

```js
import { generate } from "yuku-codegen";
import { parse } from "yuku-parser";

const { code, map, diagnostics } = generate(parse("const x = 1 + 2;").program);
```

It is plain JavaScript, prints any ESTree AST whichever parser produced it, and runs 2.6x faster than `@babel/generator`, or 3x with source maps on. Numbers, bigints, and regular expression flags print from their `raw` lexeme when present, so delete `raw` after changing one of those values.

```ts
interface GenerateResult {
  code: string;
  diagnostics: Diagnostic[]; // empty on a clean run
  map: SourceMap | null;     // with sourceMap
}
```

## [Zig]($section.id('zig'))

```zig
const std = @import("std");
const parser = @import("parser");

pub fn main() !void {
    const allocator = std.heap.smp_allocator;

    var tree = try parser.parse(allocator, "const x = 1 + 2;", .{});
    defer tree.deinit();

    const result = try parser.codegen.generate(allocator, &tree, .{});
    defer result.deinit(allocator);

    std.debug.print("{s}", .{result.code});
}
```

`generate` writes into a fresh buffer and leaves the `Tree` unchanged. The returned `Result` owns its buffers, freed with `result.deinit(allocator)`. The only error it returns is `error.OutOfMemory`.

```zig
pub const Result = struct {
    code: []const u8,
    diagnostics: []const Diagnostic, // each a message, start, and end
    map: ?SourceMap = null,
};
```

## [Options]($section.id('options'))

```js
generate(program, { strip: true, minify: true });
```

```zig
const result = try parser.codegen.generate(allocator, &tree, .{ .strip = true, .minify = true });
```

| JavaScript  | Zig          | Default    | Description                                                          |
| ----------- | ------------ | ---------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `strip`     | `strip`      | `false`    | Drop TypeScript-only syntax. See [Type stripping](#type-stripping).  |
| `minify`    | `minify`     | `false`    | Minify the output. See [Minification](#minification).                |
| `format`    | `format`     | `pretty`   | `pretty`, or `compact` for only the separators the grammar requires. |
| `indent`    | `indent`     | `2`        | Spaces per level in pretty format, from 0 to 255.                    |
| `quotes`    | `quotes`     | `preserve` | Quote style for string literals. See [Quotes](#quotes).              |
| `comments`  | `comments`   | `some`     | Which attached comments to emit. See [Comments](#comments).          |
| `sourceMap` | `source_map` | none       | Emit a Source Map V3. See [Source maps](#source-maps).               |

Every transformation is an independent flag, so they compose freely.

## [Type stripping]($section.id('type-stripping'))

`strip` prints a TypeScript AST as JavaScript. It is not a regex pass or a separate transform. The codegen skips TypeScript-only nodes and fields during its one walk, so every boundary case (comments, generic calls, nested template literal types) is a node the parser already classified.

```js
generate(parse(`const x: number = 1;`, { lang: "ts" }).program, { strip: true }).code;
// "const x = 1;"
```

Types, interfaces, type aliases, generics, type assertions, `satisfies`, non-null `!`, `declare`, and `abstract` strip cleanly. A few TypeScript features emit runtime values: `enum`, `namespace`, `module`, `export =`, `import = require()`, and parameter properties. Converting them is transpilation, not stripping, so each one is reported in `diagnostics` and left out, a parameter property keeps its plain parameter, and the rest of the file is still emitted. Their ambient forms (`declare enum`, `declare namespace`, `declare module`, `import type X = require(...)`) carry no runtime and strip silently.

## [Minification]($section.id('minification'))

`minify` applies size-reducing syntax rewrites.

- `true` and `false` become `!0` and `!1`.
- Numeric literals take their shortest form (`1000000` becomes `1e6`, `0.5` becomes `.5`).
- `obj["foo"]` becomes `obj.foo` when the key is a valid identifier.
- `{ "foo": x }` becomes `{ foo: x }` when safe.
- `</script`, `<!--`, and `-->` are escaped in strings and untagged template literals, so the output is safe to inline in a `<script>` tag. Tagged templates keep their raw text, since the tag reads it.

In JavaScript, `minify: true` also emits compact whitespace and the shortest quotes. An object picks the switches, `whitespace`, `syntax`, and `quotes`, and enabled switches override `format` and `quotes`.

```js
generate(program, { minify: { syntax: true } }); // rewrites only, readable output
```

In Zig, `minify` is the syntax rewrites alone. Combine it with `.format = .compact` and `.quotes = .shortest` for maximum minification.

## [Quotes]($section.id('quotes'))

| Value      | Behavior                                                               |
| ---------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `preserve` | Keep each literal's source quote style, re-escaping the content.       |
| `double`   | Force double quotes.                                                   |
| `single`   | Force single quotes.                                                   |
| `shortest` | Pick whichever quote needs fewer escapes per literal, double on a tie. |

## [Comments]($section.id('comments'))

Comments print from the nodes they are [attached](/parser/ast/#comments) to, so parse with `attachComments: true` (`.comments = .attached` in Zig) to keep them.

| Value   | Behavior                                                                            |
| ------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `some`  | Legal headers, JSDoc, and `@`/`#` annotations, the bundler convention. The default. |
| `all`   | Every comment. `true` in JavaScript.                                                |
| `none`  | No comments. `false` in JavaScript.                                                 |
| `line`  | `// ...` only.                                                                      |
| `block` | `/* ... */` only.                                                                   |

```js
const { program } = parse(`// hello\nconst x = 1;`, { attachComments: true });
generate(program, { comments: true }).code;
// "// hello\nconst x = 1;"
```

## [Source maps]($section.id('source-maps'))

Pass the original source to emit a Source Map V3 alongside the code.

```js
import { writeFileSync } from "node:fs";

const { code, map } = generate(program, {
  sourceMap: { source, file: "out.js", sourceFileName: "in.js", sourcesContent: source },
});

writeFileSync("out.js", `${code}\n//# sourceMappingURL=out.js.map`);
writeFileSync("out.js.map", JSON.stringify(map));
```

```zig
const result = try parser.codegen.generate(allocator, &tree, .{
    .source_map = .{
        .source = source,
        .file = "out.js",
        .source_file_name = "in.js",
        .sources_content = source,
    },
});
defer result.deinit(allocator);

const map = result.map.?; // set whenever a source was given
```

| JavaScript       | Zig                | Description                                       |
| ---------------- | ------------------ | ------------------------------------------------- |
| `source`         | `source`           | The original source text. Required to emit a map. |
| `file`           | `file`             | Output filename, embedded as `file`.              |
| `sourceFileName` | `source_file_name` | Source filename, the single entry of `sources`.   |
| `sourceRoot`     | `source_root`      | Embedded as `sourceRoot`.                         |
| `sourcesContent` | `sources_content`  | The single entry of `sourcesContent`.             |

The map is the Source Map V3 wire format, ready to serialize, with the same fields in snake_case in Zig. Columns are 0-indexed UTF-16 code units, the convention of browser devtools and source map libraries.
